# Working notes for whoever picks this up next. Not documentation — the READMEs
# and docs/ are. This is the stuff that would otherwise have to be rediscovered.

test counts: 334 default / 387 with tls + cloud-http

## Advanced phases

completed:
- A15 Observability API: TrafficStats shared out of the dispatcher task,
  ClientRequest::Stats, NodeSummary enriched, same numbers on /metrics
- A16 TUI: crates/aether-tui (lib + bin), ratatui 0.29 with its re-exported
  crossterm. Verified by rendering real frames against a live 2-agent mesh;
  the README screenshots are those frames, not mock-ups.
- packaging: .github/workflows/release.yml builds all three binaries for
  6 targets on a v* tag. Distro packages (deb/brew/AUR) not done.

- A2 priority + queue: aether-core Priority (5 levels), controller queue.rs
  (linear scan, not a heap — it re-ranks on every pop, which ageing needs),
  run_dispatcher_with, QueueStats on MeshState, `priority` on Submit, TUI
  form field + queue panel. Live: 400 background tasks queued 381 deep, a
  critical submitted 250 ms in finished 57th of 401.

next: A3 queue policies — timeout, max size, rejection policy (Reject /
DropOldest / DropLowestPriority). The queue is currently unbounded.

not started: A1 real-network bench, A2/A3 priority queue (no queue exists at
all — submit dispatches immediately), A4 QUIC, A6 cost, A7 spot, A8/A9 DAG,
A10/A11 autoscale, A12/A13 checkpoint, A14 OTel, A17-A23, A24-A26 bench

## Verified by actually running it, not just by tests

- three labelled agents + controller on this machine; examples/09 output in its
  README is real, including the refusal when nothing satisfies the constraints
- examples/10 (MeshExecutor) numbers are real, including the half where the mesh
  loses to a thread pool
- Java and C# SDKs compiled and run against a live mesh; their README output is
  what they printed
- /metrics and /healthz scraped from a running controller
- both sample TOML files loaded by the real binaries

## Things that cost time to find out

- reqwest 0.12 (rustls-tls), hmac 0.12, sha2 0.10 — newer majors changed APIs
- rcgen needs the x509-parser feature to sign with an existing CA
- bincode 2 renamed DeserializeError to DecodeError
- wasmtime's Store<T> requires 'static, so WASM inputs are Arc<[u8]> not &[u8]
- tokio's BufReader has no by_ref(); cap the reader with .take() instead
- PowerShell's Get-Content -Raw / -replace mangles UTF-8 em-dashes and can eat
  newlines. Use python or the Edit tool for source files.
- sysinfo's first CPU sample after construction is arbitrary, not 0.0 — on
  Windows it reports 100%. local_node_info sleeps one interval before sampling.
- cargo test -p aether-agent --all-features fails: it enables the agent's tls
  feature but not the controller's. Test the workspace, not the crate.
- Go toolchain lives in the scratchpad (not committed): go1.24.0 windows-amd64

## Deliberate design decisions worth not re-litigating

- the telemetry endpoint reports aggregates only; a per-node label would make an
  unauthenticated port into an inventory of the network (test pins this)
- a blob larger than the whole storage budget is stored anyway — refusing it
  would fail work the mesh already decided to run here
- heartbeat backoff is capped by the controller's declared eviction window, not
  by anything the agent chooses; an agent told nothing does not back off at all
- MeshExecutor refuses Python callables rather than running them locally
- cloud adapters read platform liveness only; utilisation comes from heartbeats

## Follow-ups deferred on purpose

- the queue is unbounded and nothing times out in it. That is A3, not an
  oversight — but do not ship without it.
- `run_dispatcher` still dispatches one task at a time. The queue makes that
  survivable rather than fixing it; concurrent dispatch needs Controller to
  stop being &mut-per-task.

- the 5 SDKs ignore the new NodeSummary fields (address, latency_ms,
  bandwidth, datasets_held, bytes_held, connected) and have no stats() call.
  Backward compatible, verified live. Mechanical to add; not this phase.

## Open work

